WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.141
- Not every idea's a good idea,

00:00:02.141 --> 00:00:04.042
not every idea matters,

00:00:04.042 --> 00:00:06.508
and not every idea should be pursued.

00:00:06.508 --> 00:00:09.267
Carpe diem, seize the day!

00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:11.900
The opportunities are there

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.658
but you got to reach out and pick them up.

00:00:14.658 --> 00:00:16.330
Smart people love to be critical,

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:18.587
they love to be negative,
they love to find fault,

00:00:18.587 --> 00:00:20.384
and you have to to succeed.

00:00:20.384 --> 00:00:22.913
You can have a brilliant
idea for your startup.

00:00:22.913 --> 00:00:25.755
You've got to become a storyteller.

00:00:25.755 --> 00:00:27.197
- He's an American businessman

00:00:27.197 --> 00:00:29.496
who a was the CEO of Microsoft.

00:00:29.496 --> 00:00:31.669
He's the owner of the
Los Angeles Clippers.

00:00:31.669 --> 00:00:35.431
He has an estimated net
worth of $27.8 billion.

00:00:35.431 --> 00:00:37.458
He's Steve Ballmer, and here's my take

00:00:37.458 --> 00:00:39.443
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:39.443 --> 00:00:41.073
Rule number three is me personal favorite

00:00:41.073 --> 00:00:42.534
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:42.534 --> 00:00:43.851
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:43.851 --> 00:00:45.042
Also, as Steve is talking,

00:00:45.042 --> 00:00:46.609
if he says something that
is really meaningful to you,

00:00:46.609 --> 00:00:49.472
make sure to leave it in the
comments but quotes around it

00:00:49.472 --> 00:00:51.708
so that other people can be
inspired by the comment as well.

00:00:51.708 --> 00:00:53.694
(softly swishing)

00:00:53.694 --> 00:00:55.432
(softly thuds)

00:00:55.432 --> 00:00:58.253
- Number one for me, life lesson,

00:00:58.253 --> 00:01:00.003
is that ideas matter.

00:01:01.639 --> 00:01:03.227
Now people say, of course ideas matter.

00:01:03.227 --> 00:01:08.054
What kind of stupid life
lesson is it that ideas matter?

00:01:08.054 --> 00:01:10.395
The truth of the matter
is this is both under

00:01:10.395 --> 00:01:12.673
and over appreciated.

00:01:12.673 --> 00:01:15.981
Most people think every idea's a good idea

00:01:15.981 --> 00:01:18.489
as long as they had it.

00:01:18.489 --> 00:01:19.826
The truth of the matter is,

00:01:19.826 --> 00:01:21.874
not every idea's a good idea,

00:01:21.874 --> 00:01:23.650
not every idea matters,

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:26.513
and not every idea should be pursued.

00:01:26.513 --> 00:01:28.164
And if you're in a field like I am,

00:01:28.164 --> 00:01:30.672
or I was, of innovation,

00:01:30.672 --> 00:01:32.866
not every idea is a good idea,

00:01:32.866 --> 00:01:35.604
even if it strikes you
that way on first blush.

00:01:35.604 --> 00:01:38.133
In fact, most people are
lucky to have one idea

00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:40.766
that really matters in their whole life.

00:01:40.766 --> 00:01:42.500
That's not a criticism.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:45.969
But earth-shattering,
life-changing ideas are very few

00:01:45.969 --> 00:01:47.349
and far between.

00:01:47.349 --> 00:01:51.424
Research insights, investment
insights, innovations,

00:01:51.424 --> 00:01:54.830
a good idea needs to be
cherished and nurtured.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:58.446
- I apologize in advance
for the sports reference.

00:01:58.446 --> 00:02:01.120
But I would say get in the weight room.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.210
The weight room of life.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:04.924
What do you do in a weight room?

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:07.641
If you're an athlete or a kind

00:02:07.641 --> 00:02:10.378
of retired senior citizen like me,

00:02:10.378 --> 00:02:11.378
you prepare.

00:02:12.677 --> 00:02:16.042
You prepare, you build new capabilities.

00:02:16.042 --> 00:02:19.114
You get stronger, you get faster.

00:02:19.114 --> 00:02:21.747
You figure out how to get leverage.

00:02:21.747 --> 00:02:23.440
And yet, in your business lives,

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.347
those are precisely the right messages.

00:02:27.347 --> 00:02:30.273
One of the things that I
think has made Microsoft

00:02:30.273 --> 00:02:31.924
the company that it is today

00:02:31.924 --> 00:02:35.184
is that we weren't afraid
to get in the weight room.

00:02:35.184 --> 00:02:39.991
In the late 80s, we had plenty
of customers, investors,

00:02:39.991 --> 00:02:44.944
tell us we would, "Never
be an enterprise company."

00:02:44.944 --> 00:02:46.615
Today, many people think the bulk

00:02:46.615 --> 00:02:50.022
of the $350 billion of value in Microsoft

00:02:50.022 --> 00:02:52.885
is in our enterprise business.

00:02:52.885 --> 00:02:54.724
We weren't afraid to sit there,

00:02:54.724 --> 00:02:58.694
even before we had it
figured out, and just work.

00:02:58.694 --> 00:03:02.769
To prepare, to get better,
to build new capabilities.

00:03:02.769 --> 00:03:04.358
And whether we're talking about

00:03:04.358 --> 00:03:06.928
what you need to do for your startup,

00:03:06.928 --> 00:03:09.227
for the hospital that you join,

00:03:09.227 --> 00:03:11.463
or for yourself personally,

00:03:11.463 --> 00:03:13.720
you have to build new capabilities,

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:16.708
new quickness, and new leverage.

00:03:16.708 --> 00:03:18.318
There's a Latin expression

00:03:18.318 --> 00:03:21.013
which I think is great, I love it,

00:03:21.013 --> 00:03:23.647
was in a now very old movie,

00:03:23.647 --> 00:03:26.886
called, The Dead Poet Society.

00:03:26.886 --> 00:03:31.755
But the line in the movie was
carpe diem, seize the day!

00:03:31.755 --> 00:03:33.949
The opportunities are there,

00:03:33.949 --> 00:03:36.833
but you got to reach out and pick them up.

00:03:36.833 --> 00:03:38.818
You've got to grab at them.

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:40.657
Some of you may have already done that

00:03:40.657 --> 00:03:42.601
at the U in your classes,

00:03:42.601 --> 00:03:45.088
in other students that you met,

00:03:45.088 --> 00:03:48.870
in your extracurricular
activities, but grab them.

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:50.250
Don't be afraid to make a mistake

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:53.489
'cause you know what you can
do if you grab the wrong one?

00:03:53.489 --> 00:03:56.373
Drop it and pick up another one.

00:03:56.373 --> 00:03:58.456
It's okay, seize the day.

00:03:59.946 --> 00:04:02.529
I think back of all of the luck

00:04:04.462 --> 00:04:07.262
but also the times that
were in front of me

00:04:07.262 --> 00:04:08.679
to seize the day.

00:04:09.958 --> 00:04:12.905
I don't know what got me to
drop out of business school

00:04:12.905 --> 00:04:14.848
and come to Microsoft.

00:04:14.848 --> 00:04:17.732
My parents thought I was a wack job.

00:04:17.732 --> 00:04:20.428
Neither one of them graduated college

00:04:20.428 --> 00:04:23.939
and they thought this
was really a wild idea.

00:04:23.939 --> 00:04:26.356
I was lucky I seized the day.

00:04:27.575 --> 00:04:31.325
Microsoft, one day, some
guys fly in from IBM

00:04:33.552 --> 00:04:35.015
and all of a sudden, we figure out

00:04:35.015 --> 00:04:38.567
we could actually provide
all the software they need

00:04:38.567 --> 00:04:42.329
for this thing that became
the personal computer.

00:04:42.329 --> 00:04:45.526
People, Bill, Paul, they had the wisdom

00:04:45.526 --> 00:04:49.999
to seize the day when that
opportunity presented itself.

00:04:49.999 --> 00:04:52.318
And yet, when you think
of all the opportunities,

00:04:52.318 --> 00:04:54.638
when I think back of all
the opportunities I've had,

00:04:54.638 --> 00:04:56.665
the one that was most important

00:04:56.665 --> 00:04:59.415
was an opportunity I got in 1969.

00:05:00.698 --> 00:05:03.248
Sitting in my junior high school class,

00:05:03.248 --> 00:05:05.087
I was in a public junior high,

00:05:05.087 --> 00:05:07.511
not very stimulated at the time,

00:05:07.511 --> 00:05:09.594
and over the loudspeaker,

00:05:11.076 --> 00:05:13.465
they said that a private high school

00:05:13.465 --> 00:05:15.617
in our area that I never heard of

00:05:15.617 --> 00:05:19.295
was giving scholarship tests that weekend.

00:05:19.295 --> 00:05:21.218
I told my mother I wanted to take 'em,

00:05:21.218 --> 00:05:23.976
she said, "That's fine, as
long as you get a scholarship

00:05:23.976 --> 00:05:26.129
"'cause we can't afford
to send you there."

00:05:26.129 --> 00:05:28.929
But that's really where I got switched on.

00:05:28.929 --> 00:05:31.918
Switched on in math,
switched on personally.

00:05:31.918 --> 00:05:36.085
Energized in a way that never,
never could be turned back.

00:05:36.996 --> 00:05:40.507
And I'm very thankful
for that opportunity.

00:05:40.507 --> 00:05:43.808
Yes, there's a lot of luck in opportunity,

00:05:43.808 --> 00:05:47.027
but there's a lot of seizing the day,

00:05:47.027 --> 00:05:51.959
and I encourage you all to
reach out and carpe diem,

00:05:51.959 --> 00:05:55.846
really seize the opportunities
that are in front of you.

00:05:55.846 --> 00:05:56.679
Optimism.

00:05:57.664 --> 00:06:00.109
Smart people love to be negative.

00:06:00.109 --> 00:06:02.867
That's my basic summary of life,

00:06:02.867 --> 00:06:04.686
smart people love to be critical,

00:06:04.686 --> 00:06:06.859
they love to be negative,
they love to find fault,

00:06:06.859 --> 00:06:09.116
and you have to succeed.

00:06:09.116 --> 00:06:12.439
On the other hand, if you
really want to be successful,

00:06:12.439 --> 00:06:14.737
you have to stay optimistic.

00:06:14.737 --> 00:06:18.515
Colin Powell, who in the Joint
Chief of Staff for the US,

00:06:18.515 --> 00:06:20.197
has written books on leadership.

00:06:20.197 --> 00:06:22.141
But he's got one line I love.

00:06:22.141 --> 00:06:25.558
I love, "Optimism is a force multiplier."

00:06:26.613 --> 00:06:28.410
Some of the folks I was sitting down with,

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:31.106
I don't know what you call it,

00:06:31.106 --> 00:06:33.029
the other part of the union,

00:06:33.029 --> 00:06:35.098
some of the union members
before we started.

00:06:35.098 --> 00:06:38.525
You know, we were talking
about goofy Youtube speeches

00:06:38.525 --> 00:06:43.045
and videos that some of them
have watched of me speaking.

00:06:43.045 --> 00:06:43.878
And, why?

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:47.016
I am optimistic, and leaders,

00:06:47.016 --> 00:06:50.046
whether they're optimistic or
not, you better project it.

00:06:50.046 --> 00:06:52.658
Because if you're not
leading with optimism,

00:06:52.658 --> 00:06:53.724
if you don't believe,

00:06:53.724 --> 00:06:55.918
nobody else is going to go your direction.

00:06:55.918 --> 00:06:58.280
Nobody's going to be with
you, nobody's going to follow.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:00.704
Nobody's really going to buy in.

00:07:00.704 --> 00:07:02.815
There is an advantage to being,

00:07:02.815 --> 00:07:05.782
what I like to call now, hardcore.

00:07:05.782 --> 00:07:08.582
Hardcore, I don't know
quite how to describe it.

00:07:08.582 --> 00:07:11.822
It's a combination of
tenacious and dedicated,

00:07:11.822 --> 00:07:15.989
and passionate and committed,
something like that, hardcore.

00:07:17.506 --> 00:07:19.951
I learned about being hardcore

00:07:19.951 --> 00:07:22.793
from math concentrators at Harvard.

00:07:22.793 --> 00:07:24.304
(audience laughing)

00:07:24.304 --> 00:07:26.644
I'm not going to ask whether we have

00:07:26.644 --> 00:07:29.236
any math concentrators
here, but I would say

00:07:29.236 --> 00:07:31.472
that's where I got kind of my lesson.

00:07:31.472 --> 00:07:34.356
I mean, people could just go crazy,

00:07:34.356 --> 00:07:37.114
focus, and it wasn't just
the people who were genius.

00:07:37.114 --> 00:07:41.189
But working, and working,
and working, and working.

00:07:41.189 --> 00:07:43.175
I'd sit in my room
working on a problem set

00:07:43.175 --> 00:07:44.805
and after three hours, I'm a little ADD,

00:07:44.805 --> 00:07:47.438
I just couldn't take it.
(softly mimics rocket rumbling)

00:07:47.438 --> 00:07:51.325
The most hardcore people could
just power through anything

00:07:51.325 --> 00:07:53.749
and the advantage of being that tenacious

00:07:53.749 --> 00:07:55.964
and dedicated and committed,

00:07:55.964 --> 00:07:59.028
I claim it helped us at Microsoft.

00:07:59.028 --> 00:08:00.762
It's fundamental at Microsoft.

00:08:00.762 --> 00:08:03.145
But I learned it here at Harvard.

00:08:03.145 --> 00:08:07.178
You really need to learn
how to tell a story.

00:08:07.178 --> 00:08:09.581
Particularly with MBAs,

00:08:09.581 --> 00:08:12.382
and when I say MBAs, I'm
really speaking generally

00:08:12.382 --> 00:08:14.325
to the group, but I'm being parochial

00:08:14.325 --> 00:08:16.310
'cause I can't come up
with a general expression

00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:17.836
for MBA tonight.

00:08:17.836 --> 00:08:20.929
But I do find that a lot of people coming

00:08:20.929 --> 00:08:24.607
out of school focus a
lot of what we should do,

00:08:24.607 --> 00:08:28.975
and don't spend enough time
learning how to tell the story

00:08:28.975 --> 00:08:32.392
of why their idea brings value to others.

00:08:34.199 --> 00:08:38.366
The number of storytellers in
the world is very, very small.

00:08:40.636 --> 00:08:44.920
I don't know what exactly
I will do next in my life.

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:46.759
I don't know, I'm not sure.

00:08:46.759 --> 00:08:48.744
I don't have an idea.

00:08:48.744 --> 00:08:52.213
But I've been spending a lot
of time in the weight room

00:08:52.213 --> 00:08:55.097
reading up on the US government budget,

00:08:55.097 --> 00:08:57.458
healthcare cost, and debt.

00:08:57.458 --> 00:09:00.781
And I say that to people, nobody knows

00:09:00.781 --> 00:09:04.397
what the heck I'm doing, I
can't explain, I don't know.

00:09:04.397 --> 00:09:08.221
Earlier this week, I came
up with a way to explain it.

00:09:08.221 --> 00:09:10.185
As CEO of Microsoft,

00:09:10.185 --> 00:09:14.352
I was responsible for signing
on something called, our 10-Q.

00:09:15.598 --> 00:09:17.834
Every quarter, you had to publish it

00:09:17.834 --> 00:09:20.279
and it had to tell a
story of your company.

00:09:20.279 --> 00:09:22.431
You have to get up in front of analysts,

00:09:22.431 --> 00:09:24.939
I didn't do that very
well, but nonetheless,

00:09:24.939 --> 00:09:28.387
I did it well, I just didn't
do it very frequently.

00:09:28.387 --> 00:09:31.230
Nonetheless, you got out ever quarter,

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:33.549
you put out a statement,
you explained yourself.

00:09:33.549 --> 00:09:35.493
You told the story,
what's good, what's bad,

00:09:35.493 --> 00:09:38.063
what's working, what's not working.

00:09:38.063 --> 00:09:41.950
The US government doesn't put
out a quarterly statement.

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:45.148
There's no regular story
from our government

00:09:45.148 --> 00:09:48.303
about what's working
and what's not working.

00:09:48.303 --> 00:09:51.793
Where cost really is
going and where it's not.

00:09:51.793 --> 00:09:54.698
What the president is trying to do.

00:09:54.698 --> 00:09:57.143
The state of the union is a long list.

00:09:57.143 --> 00:10:00.194
And whether you like
republicans or democrats,

00:10:00.194 --> 00:10:04.666
you still don't get a
simple and succinct story.

00:10:04.666 --> 00:10:07.613
I started telling people
this week I want to work.

00:10:07.613 --> 00:10:09.452
From publicly available information,

00:10:09.452 --> 00:10:12.942
I'm putting together a
10-Q for the government.

00:10:12.942 --> 00:10:15.429
People said, "Oh, we get that."

00:10:15.429 --> 00:10:19.629
That's a story, a story
that you can start telling

00:10:19.629 --> 00:10:21.385
and people understand.

00:10:21.385 --> 00:10:23.955
You could have a brilliant
idea for your startup,

00:10:23.955 --> 00:10:27.403
you've got to become a storyteller.

00:10:27.403 --> 00:10:30.538
Some of you will say, "I'm not
an expert at telling stories

00:10:30.538 --> 00:10:33.129
"and telling yarns," you're
not great public speakers.

00:10:33.129 --> 00:10:35.574
That is not what I'm talking about.

00:10:35.574 --> 00:10:39.649
I'm talking about a
clear, simple, concise way

00:10:39.649 --> 00:10:43.816
to convey the value of the
ideas that are in your head.

00:10:45.104 --> 00:10:47.298
I think it's one of the most

00:10:47.298 --> 00:10:50.412
underappreciated skills in business,

00:10:50.412 --> 00:10:51.937
it's one of the skills

00:10:51.937 --> 00:10:55.854
that we did talk about
explicitly at Microsoft,

00:10:57.350 --> 00:11:02.345
and if I could wish you one
new capability to go build,

00:11:02.345 --> 00:11:06.503
it would be the capability
of storytelling.

00:11:06.503 --> 00:11:11.352
You better stay on top of
yourself and really measure.

00:11:11.352 --> 00:11:13.003
Unless people measure themselves,

00:11:13.003 --> 00:11:14.277
you don't get any better.

00:11:14.277 --> 00:11:16.911
You cannot succeed unless you are

00:11:16.911 --> 00:11:20.484
really holding yourself
accountable and owning outcomes.

00:11:20.484 --> 00:11:23.263
Believe me, I've seen plenty
of people at Microsoft,

00:11:23.263 --> 00:11:25.792
various places in the tech
industry, brilliant people,

00:11:25.792 --> 00:11:29.533
who when it came time to execute
and actually count success,

00:11:29.533 --> 00:11:31.366
eh, maybe not so much.

00:11:32.647 --> 00:11:34.339
Maybe not so much.

00:11:34.339 --> 00:11:36.346
In order to do that,

00:11:36.346 --> 00:11:38.456
you have to actually
know what the goal is.

00:11:38.456 --> 00:11:41.570
If the goal is to get
the 450 million users

00:11:41.570 --> 00:11:45.737
and then sell to Facebook,
WhatsApp did a hell of a job.

00:11:46.586 --> 00:11:49.240
I mean that, it's not a...

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:52.082
It's a hell of a job,
19,000,000,055 people,

00:11:52.082 --> 00:11:53.189
that's a hell of a job.

00:11:53.189 --> 00:11:56.011
But you have to decide
what outcome do you want?

00:11:56.011 --> 00:11:59.145
Bill Gates was offered $7.5 million

00:11:59.145 --> 00:12:02.635
to sell all of Microsoft in 1979.

00:12:02.635 --> 00:12:05.582
He would have been a rich
guy if he had taken it.

00:12:05.582 --> 00:12:08.152
Richer if he didn't, but a rich guy.

00:12:08.152 --> 00:12:10.200
But even putting that aside,

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:12.959
the impact on society
wouldn't have been the same.

00:12:12.959 --> 00:12:15.780
We had discussions with
Facebook about buying Facebook,

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:19.061
but Zuckerberg knew what
he viewed as an outcome,

00:12:19.061 --> 00:12:21.611
that he really desired and was willing

00:12:21.611 --> 00:12:24.265
to put in blood, sweat, and
tears just like we were.

00:12:24.265 --> 00:12:28.862
So, execution and measurement
are hugely important

00:12:28.862 --> 00:12:32.018
and yet people let
themselves off the hook.

00:12:32.018 --> 00:12:34.839
In life, you want to be
your own quarterback.

00:12:34.839 --> 00:12:36.991
See the playing field.

00:12:36.991 --> 00:12:40.022
Many people like to get
myopically tied into one thing

00:12:40.022 --> 00:12:43.324
and they have a hard time stepping back.

00:12:43.324 --> 00:12:45.873
Being CEO of Microsoft is this amazing,

00:12:45.873 --> 00:12:47.775
wonderful opportunity.

00:12:47.775 --> 00:12:50.366
Turns out everybody in the world wants

00:12:50.366 --> 00:12:52.268
to tell you what to do.

00:12:52.268 --> 00:12:54.567
And they'll educate you.

00:12:54.567 --> 00:12:56.677
You want to learn about
any field of technology,

00:12:56.677 --> 00:12:58.976
as long as you don't
act like a know-it-all,

00:12:58.976 --> 00:13:01.150
somebody will educate you.

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:04.180
And you have a unique
ability to see broadly

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:05.998
across the technology field.

00:13:05.998 --> 00:13:08.401
Right now, I'm spending a lot of my time

00:13:08.401 --> 00:13:11.557
studying government and the economy.

00:13:11.557 --> 00:13:14.900
Turns out the number of
places you can actually read

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:17.471
about the totality of government

00:13:17.471 --> 00:13:20.041
in the United States is almost zero.

00:13:20.041 --> 00:13:23.719
What is the total spend in
government in this country?

00:13:23.719 --> 00:13:25.872
And what does it get spent on?

00:13:25.872 --> 00:13:28.902
There's no government
report on that topic.

00:13:28.902 --> 00:13:30.992
The best cite happens to come

00:13:30.992 --> 00:13:33.980
from some random blogger who I met,

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:35.548
who I think is great, by the way,

00:13:35.548 --> 00:13:38.327
but I found him through
a Bing search engine,

00:13:38.327 --> 00:13:42.862
not surprisingly.
(audience laughing)

00:13:42.862 --> 00:13:45.202
That's my only plug for the day.

00:13:45.202 --> 00:13:46.465
I'm still a shareholder.

00:13:46.465 --> 00:13:48.400
But seeing the field.

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:49.233
Have fun.

00:13:50.625 --> 00:13:52.610
You're not going to have fun
every minute of every day.

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:55.035
I don't care what job you take, you know,

00:13:55.035 --> 00:13:59.193
any job you take, I don't care
how well rewarded you are,

00:13:59.193 --> 00:14:01.826
I don't care anything about that.

00:14:01.826 --> 00:14:03.958
It won't happen all the time,

00:14:03.958 --> 00:14:07.625
but you better be having
fun in what you do.

00:14:08.744 --> 00:14:13.195
The truth is a lot of success
won't just come from ideas

00:14:13.195 --> 00:14:16.727
and being hardcore and
being in the weight room.

00:14:16.727 --> 00:14:20.154
A lot of your success is
going to come from luck.

00:14:20.154 --> 00:14:22.557
I know that was true in my case,

00:14:22.557 --> 00:14:24.375
and I know, frankly, it's the case

00:14:24.375 --> 00:14:27.197
of almost every successful entrepreneur

00:14:27.197 --> 00:14:31.293
I've met in the tech business
in the last 34 years.

00:14:31.293 --> 00:14:35.033
People will make it seem like
it was the grand master plan,

00:14:35.033 --> 00:14:39.464
in all cases, of an all-knowing
and all-seeing genius.

00:14:39.464 --> 00:14:43.631
That, and a lot of luck is
what really leads to success.

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:48.053
So given that you're going to
have to have a lot of luck,

00:14:48.053 --> 00:14:50.435
you better be having a lot of fun.

00:14:50.435 --> 00:14:53.988
Don't take the next job or
don't do the next startup

00:14:53.988 --> 00:14:55.388
because you should.

00:14:55.388 --> 00:14:57.331
Do it because you want to.

00:14:57.331 --> 00:14:59.923
Because you can think of nothing better,

00:14:59.923 --> 00:15:02.702
more exciting, more energizing

00:15:02.702 --> 00:15:04.040
to get up everyday and go do

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:07.207
than whatever it is that you selected.

00:15:10.647 --> 00:15:15.080
Be patient when you get the
first signs that it's not fun

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:17.525
because time must be measured.

00:15:17.525 --> 00:15:20.192
Constantly be improving yourself

00:15:21.140 --> 00:15:23.126
'cause only by improving yourself

00:15:23.126 --> 00:15:25.989
will you maybe find that next idea.

00:15:25.989 --> 00:15:27.974
I'm sure there're many
people in this audience

00:15:27.974 --> 00:15:29.771
who'd like to do a startup.

00:15:29.771 --> 00:15:33.157
And yet, the first law
of doing a startup is

00:15:33.157 --> 00:15:34.808
you got to have an idea.

00:15:34.808 --> 00:15:37.733
But if you're havin' fun, if
you're building capabilities,

00:15:37.733 --> 00:15:39.489
if you're treasuring your time,

00:15:39.489 --> 00:15:42.394
if you're building your
muscle and being hardcore,

00:15:42.394 --> 00:15:45.338
the good ideas will come to you.

00:15:45.338 --> 00:15:49.005
